<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <RootNamespace>Einzel.Wpf</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>einzel-shell</AssemblyName>

    <!--
      Lets the project restore and compile on Linux, where the Windows targeting
      packs are not installed by default. It does not make it runnable there and is
      not meant to: what CI checks on Linux is that nothing below the shell has
      acquired a dependency on it, and the shell compiling is a bonus.
    -->
    <EnableWindowsTargeting>true</EnableWindowsTargeting>

    <!--
      WPF cannot run in globalization-invariant mode: its font cache constructs
      `new CultureInfo("en")` while measuring the first line of text, which throws,
      and the window dies before it is shown. Directory.Build.props turns the mode on
      for everything, so it has to come back off here.

      What that mode was protecting is unaffected. Its stated purpose is that number
      and date formatting must not vary with the host locale, because that would leak
      into CLI output (CLI-5) and into golden-file comparisons - and the way this
      codebase achieves that is by passing CultureInfo.InvariantCulture explicitly at
      every formatting and parsing site, which the shell does throughout. The build
      flag was the belt to that braces, and the braces are what hold.

      The one that matters most is the parse: a person typing a value into the model
      tree is parsed invariantly whatever their locale, because the file they are
      editing is invariant. `1,5` is refused rather than quietly read as fifteen.
    -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>false</InvariantGlobalization>

    <Description>
      The shell. Spec section 16: WPF on Windows, eleven required views. UI-1 is
      the constraint that shapes it - the shell owns layout, input, the interactive
      viewport and the update check, and owns no physics, no validation rules, no
      file format knowledge and no render output. Amendment 25 adds that every
      action here must be expressible as a CLI invocation and journalled as one, so
      a capability with no command spelling cannot be added to the window.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Einzel.Commands\Einzel.Commands.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="HelixToolkit.Wpf.SharpDX" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
